WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:04.660
So you made a fifteen part video that I responded
to with a sixteen part video because I thought

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.200
your video had sixteen parts.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.069
And then you made another fifteen part video.

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:10.340
So here are sixteen things I love about being
outside.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.180
Now Hank, as you know I am a long time opponent
of outside.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.380
Our ancestors made extraordinary sacrifices
for millennia to create inside.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.430
And then we just dishonor all of that work
by willfully going outside.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.020
But that noted, there is a lot I like about
being outside.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:29.260
Beginning with the irregularly shaped polygons
that the sky forms through the trees.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.390
I love daddy long legs.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.700
How is a creature like you even real?

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.859
This honeysuckle tree is invasive and horrible
but I love the way it bends toward the light.

00:00:36.859 --> 00:00:39.550
I love the mighty White River here in Indianapolis.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:43.429
So Hank, Indianapolis was created by politicians
who wanted to put the capital city of Indiana

00:00:43.429 --> 00:00:45.339
in the geographical middle of Indiana.

00:00:45.339 --> 00:00:48.300
And they were like "Oh that place looks good
it's on the banks of the mighty White River.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.550
And that was important in the early nineteenth
century, you had to be on some kind of navigable

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.589
waterway to like, get stuff to your town.

00:00:54.589 --> 00:00:57.640
But it turns out, that in many places the
White River is like six inches deep, which

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.979
is how Indianapolis came to be the largest
American city not on a navigable waterway!

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:06.479
I love geese and their little goslings, I
also love Ryan Gosling but that's a different

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:07.479
video.

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:08.909
I love that downy woodpecker that's in there
somewhere.

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:09.909
I love turtles.

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:10.909
Bye turtle!

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:12.939
Sorry if I scared you.

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:13.939
I love flowers.

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:18.100
Contemporary flowers are like a collaboration
among humans and plants and bees.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.360
Boy, Hank do we need to collaborate better
with plants and bees.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.620
I love bubbles and they're just not the same
inside.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.719
I love peeing outside.

00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:26.130
For the record I'm not doing it right now.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.780
I'm just saying I love that feeling, it's
like "I too am an animal!"

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.759
I love like weird swamps that feel undiscovered,
they make me feel like an explorer.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:34.970
I love snails.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.630
Also, while I have great admiration for the
hardworking chemistry majors in the artificial

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:43.159
smell business, there's nothing quite like
the actual smell of outside.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.460
I know I'm starting to sound like the nature
enthusiasts I've always reviled but these

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.530
leaves just smell so good!

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:52.510
I just got a mosquito bite on my cheek and
now I probably have West Nile virus so thanks

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:55.249
mosquitoes, for reminding me what I don't
love about outside.

00:01:55.249 --> 00:01:59.609
I love how like when I'm looking for coyotes
and great blue herons and all that kind of

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:03.950
stuff sometimes I'll just see Henry and he'll
be like "Dad, Dad, Dad, look at this moss!!"

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.160
He and Alice always remind me that with the
right level of attentiveness, everything is

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.160
amazing.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.160
I love tiny little trees.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.400
The forest is big and mostly without light
and you probably won't make it, tiny little

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.430
tree, but I wish you luck!

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.740
I love trees that lean out over the water.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.530
They're doomed but they keep going.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.970
I guess most of what I like about nature Hank,
is its ability to go on, no matter what.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.480
I find that quite inspirational.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.650
I'll see you on Friday.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:28.870
Oh.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:33.710
Gah, ruined a perfectly good outro, this guy.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:35.610
Stupid nature always tripping me.

